UID:e2c3d6eff851699a@role-environmental-land-use.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260829T070439Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Process for Authorizing Seasonal Migratory Ga
 me Bird Hunting
DESCRIPTION:The U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service (Service or we) is changing
  the administrative process for authorizing seasonal migratory game bird h
 unting in the United States. Migratory game bird hunting regulations are c
 urrently promulgated annually to provide opportunities for recreation and 
 sustenance\; aid Federal\, State\, and Tribal governments in the managemen
 t of migratory game birds\; and allow harvests at levels compatible with m
 igratory game bird population status and habitat conditions. The Service i
 s adopting a more efficient administrative process for authorizing seasona
 l migratory game bird hunting. The Service will issue a memorandum to esta
 blish the limits and authorize seasonal migratory game bird hunting once e
 very 3 years. The Service will continue to make annual decisions on harves
 t levels and will update the memorandum sooner than 3 years if changes are
  prescribed by our decision frameworks. The process eliminates the need fo
 r subsequent annual Federal regulation promulgation and rulemaking and is 
 expected to increase efficiency\; better meet State\, Tribal\, and Federal
  rulemaking constraints\; and reduce the complexity and costs. Our goal is
  to better serve State partners and the hunting public while continuing to
  meet the legal and conservation purposes of the Migratory Bird Treaty Act
 . Tribes are already authorized under a similar process.\nhttps://www.fede
 ralregister.gov/documents/2026/08/31/2026-17733/process-for-authorizing-se
 asonal-migratory-game-bird-hunting
URL:https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/08/31/2026-17733/proces
 s-for-authorizing-seasonal-migratory-game-bird-hunting
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:4909f689ae78296c@role-environmental-land-use.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260828T221633Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] National Hunting and Fishing Day - September 
 26
DESCRIPTION:WHEREAS\n\, Maine has a rich and storied tradition of hunting 
 and angling that dates back further than the state itself and carries forw
 ard to this day\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, hunting and angling continue to be an i
 ntegral component of the cultural fabric of communities throughout the sta
 te\, and over recent years have offered a growing number of participants a
 n opportunity to connect with nature on a personal level while simultaneou
 sly providing food security\, a sense of self-sufficiency\, and both menta
 l and physical health benefits\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, Maine's sportsmen and wo
 men were among the first conservationists to support the establishment of 
 the Maine Department of Inland Fisheries and Wildlife to conserve fish\, w
 ildlife and their habitat\, and through their license fees helped fund sta
 te efforts to provide for healthy and sustainable natural resources\; and\
 nWHEREAS\n\, upon realizing that license fees alone were insufficient to r
 estore and sustain healthy fish and wildlife populations\, sportsmen and w
 omen supported self-imposed excise taxes on firearms and ammunition\, fish
 ing tackle and motorboat fuels to raise additional conservation funds\; an
 d\nWHEREAS\n\, to this day\, the Maine Department of Inland Fisheries and 
 Wildlife is funded\, in part\, by sportsmen and women\, through this Amer
 ican System of Conservation Funding – a “\nuser pays - public benefit
 s\n” approach that is widely recognized as the most successful model for
  funding fish and wildlife management in the world\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, last
  year alone\, Maine's sportsmen and women generated $32 million through th
 is system to support the conservation efforts of the Maine Department of I
 nland Fisheries and Wildlife\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, 548\,194 hunters and angle
 rs support the state's economy through their spending\, contributing to th
 e $3.9 billion spent on outdoor activities in Maine last year\;\nNOW THERE
 FORE\n\, be it resolved that I\, Janet T. Mills\, Governor of the State of
  Maine\, do hereby proclaim September 26\, 2026 as\nNational Hunting and \
 nhttp://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/official_documents/proclamations/2026
 -08-national-hunting-and-fishing-day-september-26
URL:http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/official_documents/proclamations/2
 026-08-national-hunting-and-fishing-day-september-26
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1b7120fc4ea00286@role-environmental-land-use.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260828T221612Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Federal judge blocks attempts to restrict mai
 l-in voting
DESCRIPTION:AUGUSTA\, MAINE\n– A federal judge in Boston blocked the Tru
 mp administration from interfering with states’ rights to conduct mail-i
 n voting for the upcoming November elections\, now 67 days away. Judge Ind
 ira Talwani ruled yesterday that the plaintiffs were likely to succeed in 
 proving the administration’s order was unconstitutional.\n“Last night
 ’s decision was wonderful news for Maine voters who rely on absentee vot
 ing\,”\nSecretary Bellows said\n. “The judge made clear that the state
 s\, not the federal government\, are in charge of elections. That goes to
  the heart of our legal fights in court against the Trump Administration. 
 We will continue to stand with other states and voting rights advocates ac
 ross the nation to defend the voting rights – including the right to vot
 e by mail – for every citizen.”\nIn the decision\, Judge Talwani wrote
 \, “the Constitution charges the States with election administration” 
 going on to say that no law has been passed by Congress that “would perm
 it the USPS to regulate election mail.” The judge went even further to n
 ote that with just two months to the election\, it would an expensive and 
 likely impossible task for the 2026 election\, saying\, “Plaintiff State
 s face irreparable harm where they are required to comply with a likely un
 constitutional regulation for which compliance is practically impossible.
 ”\n###\nAttachment:\nread the 08-27-26 order\nhttps://www.maine.gov/sos/
 news/federal-judge-blocks-attempts-restrict-mail-voting
URL:https://www.maine.gov/sos/news/federal-judge-blocks-attempts-restrict-
 mail-voting
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
