UID:a69175245ce950d5@role-environmental-land-use.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260825T015143Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260824
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Maine's Fall Foliage Season Set to Begin with
  Weekly Reports\, New Event Feature
DESCRIPTION:Home\nSkip to top navigation\nSkip to side navigation\nSkip to
  content\nSkip to footer\nMaine.gov\nAgencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp
 \n|\nSearch Maine.gov\nDACF Home\n→\nAbout DACF\n→\nNewsroom\n→ News
 \nMaine's Fall Foliage Season Set to Begin with Weekly Reports\, New Event
  Feature\nAugust 24\, 2026\nFirst 2026 foliage report arrives September 9\
 ; communities and organizations invited to share fall events with foliage 
 visitors\nAugusta\, MAINE - One of Maine's most anticipated seasons is jus
 t around the corner\, bringing brilliant color\, harvest traditions\, and 
 countless reasons to explore the state.\nThe Maine Department of Agricultu
 re\, Conservation and Forestry (DACF) will launch its 2026 fall foliage re
 porting season on Wednesday\, September 9\, at\nMaineFoliage.com\nand by\n
 email to subscribers\n. Reports will be issued each Wednesday throughout t
 he season\, tracking the progression of fall color from northern Maine and
  higher elevations southward toward the coast.\nThis year\, DACF is adding
  another way to help residents and visitors experience fall in Maine: orga
 nizations\, communities\, and event organizers are invited to\nsubmit fall
  events\nfor consideration for inclusion in the weekly foliage reports.\n"
 Fall foliage is more than something to see\, it's an invitation to experie
 nce Maine\,"\nsaid DACF Commissioner Amanda Beal\n. "Whether that means vi
 siting a farm\, hiking a trail\, attending a harvest festival\, exploring 
 a downtown\, or simply following a scenic by-way\, our weekly reports help
  people follow the color and discover more of Maine along the way."\n2026 
 Fall Foliage Season Preview\nDACF Maine Forest Service experts expect peak
  fall foliage to develop along its typical progression from north to south
 \, with trees at higher elevations generally developing color before those
  at lower elevations.\nMaine's tremendous diversity of tree species\, land
 scapes\, and growing conditions helps create both its spectacular fall col
 or and an extended viewing season. Northern and coastal areas offer striki
 ng con\nhttps://www.maine.gov/dacf/about/news/news.shtml?id=13360031
URL:https://www.maine.gov/dacf/about/news/news.shtml?id=13360031
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:f9c9e7e9f0c0df47@role-environmental-land-use.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260822T232240Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260811
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Response to Clean Air Act Section 176A Petiti
 on From New Hampshire
DESCRIPTION:The Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) is proposing to gran
 t a Clean Air Act (CAA or Act) petition submitted by the state of New Hamp
 shire on December 24\, 2025. The petition requests that the EPA remove the
  State of New Hampshire from the Ozone Transport Region (OTR) based on New
  Hampshire's continued attainment of the ozone National Ambient Air Qualit
 y Standards (NAAQS) and technical analyses demonstrating that the addition
 al control of emissions from the state will not significantly contribute t
 o ozone attainment in any area in the OTR. The OTR was established by the 
 1990 Clean Air Act Amendments and included the States of Connecticut\, Del
 aware\, Maine\, Maryland\, Massachusetts\, New Hampshire\, New Jersey\, Ne
 w York\, Pennsylvania\, Rhode Island\, Vermont\, the District of Columbia\
 , and portions of northern Virginia.\nhttps://www.federalregister.gov/docu
 ments/2026/08/11/2026-16331/response-to-clean-air-act-section-176a-petitio
 n-from-new-hampshire
URL:https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/08/11/2026-16331/respon
 se-to-clean-air-act-section-176a-petition-from-new-hampshire
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
